          <change when="2013-07-09T17:56:47.229-04:00" who="https://papyri.info/editor/users/james.cowey">Vote - AcceptText - I chose to ask N. Gonis for an opinion on this correction. His remarks were: &#xD;
'Correction agreed (κώμης is not needed in a listing). The scribe combined the sinusoid after tau with the lower part of the abbreviation for αὐτῆς.'&#xD;
On the basis of these two judgements I am happy to accept the reading.&#xD;
</change>
          <change when="2013-07-09T17:56:47.219-04:00" who="https://papyri.info/editor/users/berkes.lajos">Vote - DEFER - The abbreviation seems to be fairly common in 6th c. documents from Aphrodites Kome (cf. e.g.: p.berl.brash.17, 7-8). The only thing that troubles me a bit is that we don't have komes after autes as it is usually. Although I'm inclined to accept it, I'd appreciate some further judgements.</change>
